    {
      "id": "CH20",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "Echo Bat · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nRetain a measured open-before record, physically replace the section and obtain a fresh intact-after reading. Explain why a powered repeater alone cannot bridge the unchanged break before inviting Echo Bat.\n\nAfter recruitment, compare the unchanged open case with the restored continuity record. The new view separates receive-and-repeat behavior from repairing the medium; it grants no unreviewed regenerative route.\n\nA repeater regenerates a signal over a connected path. It cannot bridge the severed required conductor. Latest actual bench reading (stale; comparison only): Compatible path: 1 count; Total channel length: 95 m; Inclusive maximum: 100 m; Open required conductors: 0 count; Historical open recorded: 1 count; Repeater role distinguished: 1 count.\n\nRest Echo Bat\nBefore/after continuity and regeneration-limit inspection: Read and keep the repeater role and limits\nBefore/after continuity and regeneration-limit inspection: Conclude: a repeater needs the physical connection restored\nBefore/after continuity and regeneration-limit inspection: Measure the current bench configuration\nWhat an echo cannot fix: Read and keep the repeater role and limits\nWhat an echo cannot fix: Propose: a powered repeater alone bridges the unchanged severed conductor\nWhat an echo cannot fix: Conclude: a repeater needs the physical connection restored\nWhat an echo cannot fix: Measure the current bench configuration"
    },
    {
      "id": "CH04",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "Copperhead Snake · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nAt the Warrens and Cavern, retain the actual open-before reading, replace the diagnosed section, then obtain the intact-after reading before inviting Copperhead.\n\nAfter recruitment, review a fresh remaining-open or overlength example with Copperhead. The new view names the unsatisfied conductor or length check; it cannot repair it.\n\nContinuity and the complete channel length are separate requirements; restoring a conductor does not waive the length bound. Latest actual bench reading (stale; comparison only): Compatible path: 1 count; Total channel length: 95 m; Inclusive maximum: 100 m; Open required conductors: 0 count; Historical open recorded: 1 count; Repeater role distinguished: 1 count.\n\nRest Copperhead Snake\nContinuity comparison of a copper path: Measure the current bench configuration\nRemaining checks: Measure the current bench configuration"
    },
    {
      "id": "CH03",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "Photon the Firefly · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nAt the Gardens, inspect the connection and all supplied compatibility fields before applying an intervention. Ask Photon to join after reviewing that demonstration.\n\nAfter recruitment, ask Photon to compare the five fields of a supplied mismatch with the recorded matching pair. The new view identifies the failed field without changing it.\n\nCurrent supplied compatibility fields: Fiber/media: matches; Wavelength direction: matches; Supported rate: matches; Connector/polarity: matches; Host support: matches. These independent fields come before optical arithmetic. Inspect, intervene and recheck at the dock; this explanation does not certify a current repair.\n\nRest Photon the Firefly\nOptical inspection and compatible connection checklist: Read and keep all five supplied compatibility checks"
    },
    {
      "id": "CH16",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "SFP Hermit Crab · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nAt the Gardens, compare host support, medium, wavelength direction, rate and connector/polarity using the supplied optical pair. Review that accepted demonstration with Hermit Crab at the Vault or Shell Market before inviting it.\n\nAfter recruitment, compare an actual supplied optical mismatch with the recorded compatible pair. The new view names the unmet profile field; a matching shell or adequate power budget cannot waive it.\n\nCurrent supplied compatibility fields: Fiber/media: matches; Wavelength direction: matches; Supported rate: matches; Connector/polarity: matches; Host support: matches. These independent fields come before optical arithmetic. Inspect, intervene and recheck at the dock; this explanation does not certify a current repair.\n\nRest SFP Hermit Crab\nModule and host profile inspection: Read and keep all five supplied compatibility checks\nFirst unmatched field: Read and keep all five supplied compatibility checks"
    },
    {
      "id": "CH05",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "Single-Mode Unicorn · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nInspect the supplied compatible pair and compare its fresh received power and reserve at the Vault. Return to the Unicorn to review the demonstration and invite it.\n\nAfter recruitment, use the same declared profile to compare the sensitivity, maximum receive bound and reserve. The new view keeps all three checks distinct, including exact boundaries.\n\nRead sensitivity, maximum receive and required reserve separately. Enough power alone is not proof that every receiver bound is met. Latest actual bench reading (stale; comparison only): Receive power: -11 dBm; Sensitivity reserve: 3 dB; Sensitivity: -14 dBm; Maximum receive: -1 dBm; Required reserve: 2 dB; Inspection complete: 1 count; Transmit power: -3 dBm; Total passive loss: 8 dB.\n\nRest Single-Mode Unicorn\nInspection of a declared compatible single-mode profile: Measure the current bench configuration\nThree receiver bounds: Select passive path A: total loss 8 dB\nThree receiver bounds: Try the high-loss test path: total loss 10 dB\nThree receiver bounds: Select passive path B: total loss 9 dB\nThree receiver bounds: Measure the current bench configuration"
    },
    {
      "id": "CH06",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "Multimode Hydra · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nInspect a supplied compatible pair and compare its fresh received power and reserve. The Hydra can review either accepted passive path; its identity does not silently change the generic toy profile to multimode.\n\nAfter recruitment, review the mode explanation beside the same optical result. The new view distinguishes propagation modes from guaranteed payload channels and leaves every physical result unchanged.\n\nMultiple propagation modes do not create guaranteed independent payload lanes. This lesson uses the declared optical profile without inventing a multimode rate or reach bonus. Latest actual bench reading (stale; comparison only): Receive power: -12 dBm; Sensitivity reserve: 2 dB; Sensitivity: -14 dBm; Maximum receive: -1 dBm; Required reserve: 2 dB; Inspection complete: 1 count; Transmit power: -3 dBm; Total passive loss: 9 dB.\n\nRest Multimode Hydra\nInspection of a declared compatible multimode profile: Measure the current bench configuration\nModes, not payload lanes: Read optical exercise profile\nModes, not payload lanes: Measure the current bench configuration"
    },
    {
      "id": "CH07",
      "level": 2,
      "explanation": "Thunderbird · Level 2 · Active ally\n\nRecord compatible settings and the viable path, then measure signal and noise separately. Review the fresh sample with Thunderbird before inviting it.\n\nAfter recruitment, obtain a later sample and compare it with the earlier attributed reading. The new view labels which is current and explains the changed SNR without inferring throughput or weather causation.\n\nCompare signal and noise in the same measurement. A clear survey does not preserve a link after noise or configuration changes. Latest actual bench reading (stale; comparison only): Viable path: 1 count; Compatible settings: 1 count; Received signal: -65 dBm; Received noise: -90 dBm; Signal-to-noise ratio: 25 dB; Required signal-to-noise ratio: 20 dB.\n\nRest Thunderbird\nFresh radio observation after path/settings inspection: Measure the current bench configuration\nSample comparison: Select fictional scenario channel A at both endpoints\nSample comparison: Select fictional scenario channel B at both endpoints\nSample comparison: Reconnect the local emitter for the raised-noise scenario\nSample comparison: Isolate the local emitter and return to surveyed conditions\nSample comparison: Measure the current bench configuration"
    }
  ],
